IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `wc-family`

-- Women's Charter 1961 (Singapore) -- the division of matrimonial assets on divorce (s 112, Part 10 Chapter 4) and the
-- separation facts of irretrievable breakdown that a divorce "by consent" rests on (s 95A(1)(d), (e), (f), read with the
-- jurisdiction, three-year bar and breakdown rules already in wc-family.l4: ss 93, 94, 95, 95A).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation "version in force from 17/8/2026", as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/WC1961.txt. s 112 is in Part 10, CHAPTER 4 ("Financial provisions consequent on matrimonial
-- proceedings"). CHAPTER 4A of Part 10 (ss 121A onward) is "Financial relief consequential on FOREIGN matrimonial
-- proceedings" and is not the provision for a Singapore divorce.
--
-- WHY. Added on 11 October 2026 for REQ-0270 (event j072 of the Jun Hao scenario, 10 July 2041: a couple divorcing by consent
-- after three years apart, who rented throughout and own nothing jointly).
--
-- NOT ENCODED: the weighing of the s 112(2) matters into a percentage (the court's "just and equitable" judgment is an
-- input here, not computed: the structured approach the courts use comes from case law, not the Act); the forms of order in
-- s 112(5)-(9) beyond a list; maintenance of a wife or incapacitated husband (ss 113-118) and of children (s 127), which can
-- be ordered whether or not there is anything to divide; CPF moneys and HDB flats (their division turns on the CPF Act and
-- the Housing and Development Act as well); setting aside dispositions (s 132); Chapter 4A (foreign divorces); the
-- s 95A(6) formal requirements of a written agreement beyond the flag already in wc-family.l4; the parenting programme
-- (s 94A); the Family Justice Rules on uncontested and simplified divorce procedure.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules conventions of this row. No pipeline, no
-- coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Women's Charter 1961 -- divorce by consent and the division of matrimonial assets`

§§ `95A(1)(d), (e), (f) -- the facts a divorce "by consent" rests on`

-- s 95A(1): a marriage between X and Y "is taken to have irretrievably broken down if and only if -- ... (d) X and Y --
-- (i) have lived apart for a continuous period of 3 or more years immediately before the application for divorce; and
-- (ii) consent to a divorce being granted by the court; (e) X and Y have lived apart for a continuous period of 4 or more
-- years immediately before the application for divorce; or (f) subject to subsection (6)(c), X and Y agree that the
-- marriage has irretrievably broken down."
-- (5): living together "for a total of 6 months or less" does not break the period but "does not count towards" it.
-- (6)(c): "the court must not accept the agreement" if "there remains a reasonable possibility that X and Y might
-- reconcile". (f) came in by the Women's Charter (Amendment) Act 2022 (Act 3 of 2022) wef 1 July 2024.
-- The months that count are computed by `the months that count` in wc-family.l4 (more than 6 months together is treated as
-- breaking the period: a simplification recorded there).
DECLARE ConsentGround IS ONE OF
    `three years apart with consent, s 95A(1)(d)`
    `four years apart, s 95A(1)(e)`
    `agreement that the marriage has broken down, s 95A(1)(f)`
    `no consensual or separation fact`

GIVEN `months apart` IS A NUMBER, `months together meanwhile` IS A NUMBER, `both consent` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `a written agreement of breakdown` IS A BOOLEAN, `reconciliation reasonably possible` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A ConsentGround
DECIDE `the consensual or separation fact made out` IS
    IF `the months that count` `months apart` `months together meanwhile` >= 36 AND `both consent`
    THEN `three years apart with consent, s 95A(1)(d)`
    ELSE IF `the months that count` `months apart` `months together meanwhile` >= 48
         THEN `four years apart, s 95A(1)(e)`
         ELSE IF `a written agreement of breakdown` AND (NOT `reconciliation reasonably possible`)
              THEN `agreement that the marriage has broken down, s 95A(1)(f)`
              ELSE `no consensual or separation fact`

-- The whole path to a consent divorce, using the rules already in wc-family.l4: jurisdiction (s 93), three years since the
-- marriage or permission (s 94), a s 95A fact (here only (d), (e) or (f)), and "just and reasonable" (s 95(2)(c)).
GIVEN `domiciled in Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN, `years habitually resident` IS A NUMBER, `years married` IS A NUMBER,
      `months apart` IS A NUMBER, `months together meanwhile` IS A NUMBER, `both consent` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `a written agreement of breakdown` IS A BOOLEAN, `reconciliation reasonably possible` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `just and reasonable to grant` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a divorce on consent or separation may be granted` IF
        `the court has divorce jurisdiction` `domiciled in Singapore` `years habitually resident`
    AND `a divorce application may be filed` `years married` FALSE
    AND (NOT (`the consensual or separation fact made out` `months apart` `months together meanwhile` `both consent`
                  `a written agreement of breakdown` `reconciliation reasonably possible`
              EQUALS `no consensual or separation fact`))
    AND `just and reasonable to grant`

§§ `112(10) -- what is a matrimonial asset`

-- s 112(10): "'matrimonial asset' means -- (a) any asset acquired before the marriage by one party or both parties to the
-- marriage -- (i) ordinarily used or enjoyed by both parties or one or more of their children while the parties are residing
-- together for shelter or transportation or for household, education, recreational, social or aesthetic purposes; or
-- (ii) which has been substantially improved during the marriage by the other party or by both parties to the marriage; and
-- (b) any other asset of any nature acquired during the marriage by one party or both parties to the marriage, but does not
-- include any asset (not being a matrimonial home) that has been acquired by one party at any time by gift or inheritance and
-- that has not been substantially improved during the marriage by the other party or by both parties to the marriage."
-- WHO HOLDS TITLE IS NOT PART OF THE TEST. "by one party or both parties": an asset in one spouse's sole name (a bank
-- balance saved from salary during the marriage, a car, shares, an insurance policy) is a matrimonial asset under (b) just
-- as a jointly held one is. `held jointly` is carried on the record only to show that it changes nothing.
-- INFERENCE: a rented home is not an asset "acquired" by either party (the landlord owns it); `owned by one or both spouses`
-- is FALSE for it. Whether a tenancy, a deposit or CPF balances count is not settled by the Act as read: the deposit on a
-- lease, paid during the marriage, would be "any other asset of any nature acquired during the marriage".
-- "matrimonial home" is not defined in Part 10; it is an input.
DECLARE Asset HAS
    `what it is` IS A STRING
    `value` IS A NUMBER
    `owned by one or both spouses` IS A BOOLEAN
    `held jointly` IS A BOOLEAN
    `acquired during the marriage` IS A BOOLEAN
    `ordinarily used by the family while residing together` IS A BOOLEAN
    `substantially improved during the marriage by the other party or both` IS A BOOLEAN
    `acquired by one party by gift or inheritance` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is a matrimonial home` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the asset` IS AN Asset
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the gift or inheritance exclusion applies` IF
        `the asset`'s `acquired by one party by gift or inheritance`
    AND (NOT `the asset`'s `is a matrimonial home`)
    AND (NOT `the asset`'s `substantially improved during the marriage by the other party or both`)

GIVEN `the asset` IS AN Asset
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a matrimonial asset` IF
        `the asset`'s `owned by one or both spouses`
    AND (    `the asset`'s `acquired during the marriage`
         OR  `the asset`'s `ordinarily used by the family while residing together`
         OR  `the asset`'s `substantially improved during the marriage by the other party or both`)
    AND (NOT `the gift or inheritance exclusion applies` `the asset`)

GIVEN `the assets` IS A LIST OF Asset
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of matrimonial assets` `the assets` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the assets`
    WHEN EMPTY THEN 0
    WHEN `this one` FOLLOWED BY `the rest` THEN
        (IF `is a matrimonial asset` `this one` THEN 1 ELSE 0) + `the number of matrimonial assets` `the rest`

GIVEN `the assets` IS A LIST OF Asset
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the value of the matrimonial pool` `the assets` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the assets`
    WHEN EMPTY THEN 0
    WHEN `this one` FOLLOWED BY `the rest` THEN
        (IF `is a matrimonial asset` `this one` THEN `this one`'s `value` ELSE 0) + `the value of the matrimonial pool` `the rest`

GIVEN `the assets` IS A LIST OF Asset
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of jointly held assets` `the assets` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the assets`
    WHEN EMPTY THEN 0
    WHEN `this one` FOLLOWED BY `the rest` THEN
        (IF `this one`'s `held jointly` THEN 1 ELSE 0) + `the number of jointly held assets` `the rest`

§§ `112(1), (2), (5) -- the power to divide`

-- s 112(1): "The court has power, when granting or subsequent to the grant of a judgment of divorce, judicial separation or
-- nullity of marriage, to order the division between the parties of any matrimonial asset or the sale of any such asset and
-- the division between the parties of the proceeds of the sale of any such asset in such proportions as the court thinks
-- just and equitable."
-- The power is over "matrimonial asset[s]". With none, there is nothing for it to bite on. The Act names no particular
-- order for that case: INFERENCE, the court makes no order under s 112 (in practice recorded as no order for division);
-- maintenance (ss 113, 127) and custody are separate questions the court may still decide.
DECLARE DivisionOutcome IS ONE OF
    `no power yet: no judgment of divorce, judicial separation or nullity`
    `no matrimonial asset: no division order`
    `division in just and equitable proportions`

GIVEN `a judgment of divorce, judicial separation or nullity` IS A BOOLEAN, `the assets` IS A LIST OF Asset
GIVETH A DivisionOutcome
DECIDE `the section 112 outcome` IS
    IF NOT `a judgment of divorce, judicial separation or nullity`
    THEN `no power yet: no judgment of divorce, judicial separation or nullity`
    ELSE IF `the number of matrimonial assets` `the assets` = 0
         THEN `no matrimonial asset: no division order`
         ELSE `division in just and equitable proportions`

-- The proportion is the court's: "in such proportions as the court thinks just and equitable". It is an input (a fraction
-- between 0 and 1 for the applicant), and the encoding only multiplies.
GIVEN `the applicant's just and equitable proportion` IS A NUMBER, `the assets` IS A LIST OF Asset
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the applicant's share of the pool` IS
    `the value of the matrimonial pool` `the assets` * `the applicant's just and equitable proportion`

-- s 112(2): "It is the duty of the court ... to have regard to all the circumstances of the case, including the following
-- matters: (a) the extent of the contributions made by each party in money, property or work towards acquiring, improving or
-- maintaining the matrimonial assets; (b) any debt owing or obligation incurred or undertaken by either party for their joint
-- benefit or for the benefit of any child of the marriage; (c) the needs of the children (if any) of the marriage; (d) the
-- extent of the contributions made by each party to the welfare of the family, including looking after the home or caring
-- for the family or any aged or infirm relative or dependant of either party; (e) any agreement between the parties with
-- respect to the ownership and division of the matrimonial assets made in contemplation of divorce; (f) any period of
-- rent-free occupation or other benefit enjoyed by one party in the matrimonial home to the exclusion of the other party;
-- (g) the giving of assistance or support by one party to the other party ...; and (h) the matters referred to in
-- section 114(1) so far as they are relevant."
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`the matters the court must have regard to under section 112(2)` MEANS
    LIST "(a) contributions in money, property or work to acquiring, improving or maintaining the matrimonial assets",
         "(b) debts or obligations for the parties' joint benefit or for a child of the marriage",
         "(c) the needs of the children of the marriage",
         "(d) contributions to the welfare of the family, including home-making and caring",
         "(e) any agreement on ownership and division made in contemplation of divorce",
         "(f) rent-free occupation or other benefit of the matrimonial home to the exclusion of the other party",
         "(g) assistance or support by one party to the other, including in an occupation or business",
         "(h) the section 114(1) matters so far as relevant"

-- s 112(5): the court may in particular order (a) sale and division of proceeds; (b) vesting of a jointly owned asset in both
-- "in common in such shares as the court considers just and equitable"; (c) vesting in either party; (d) vesting in a trustee;
-- (e) postponing sale or vesting; (f) exclusive occupation of the matrimonial home; (g) "payment of a sum of money by one
-- party to the other party".
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`the forms of division order under section 112(5)` MEANS
    LIST "(a) sale and division of the proceeds",
         "(b) vesting a jointly owned asset in both in common in just and equitable shares",
         "(c) vesting an asset in either party",
         "(d) vesting in a person on trust",
         "(e) postponing sale or vesting",
         "(f) exclusive occupation of the matrimonial home",
         "(g) payment of a sum of money by one party to the other"
